@page "/about"

<PageTitle>@Ui.Action("about") — @Ui.App.Title</PageTitle>

<h1>@Ui.App.Title</h1>

<p class="lede">@Ui.App.Description</p>

<section class="prose">
    <h2>Where the tables come from</h2>
    <p>@((MarkupString)Ui.Licence.ContentHtml)</p>
</section>

<section class="prose">
    <h2>Licence</h2>
    @foreach (string notice in Data.Notices)
    {
        <p class="notice-line">@notice</p>
    }
</section>

<section class="prose">
    <h2>Where the data comes from</h2>

    <table class="provenance">
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th scope="col">File</th>
                <th scope="col">Describes</th>
                <th scope="col">Status</th>
                <th scope="col">Source</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            @foreach (DataFileProvenance file in Data.Provenance)
            {
                <tr>
                    <td><code>@file.Path</code></td>
                    <td>@file.Source.Describes</td>
                    <td>@(file.Source.Status ?? "—")</td>
                    <td>
                        @if (file.Source.IsDerived)
                        {
                            <span>@file.Source.Work</span>
                        }
                        else
                        {
                            <span>Written for this tool</span>
                        }
                    </td>
                </tr>
            }
        </tbody>
    </table>
</section>

<section class="prose">
    <h2>Seeds and links</h2>
    <p>
        Every site is a pure function of its seed, so the same link always rebuilds the same place.
        Locking a field records which row it landed on, and that travels in the link too — which
        means a link you paste to somebody else shows them exactly what you were looking at,
        including everything you had held in place.
    </p>
</section>

<section class="prose">
    <h2>The dice table</h2>
    <p>
        The shared dice table has no server behind it, because there is nowhere to put one. Browsers
        connect straight to each other and the dice travel between them, encrypted end to end. To
        find each other in the first place they use public relays, which carry an opaque room
        identifier and an encrypted handshake and nothing else — no names, no rolls, no results.
        Nothing is stored anywhere, by anyone. Close the tab and the table is gone.
    </p>
    <p>
        A private roll is not a roll the other players are trusted to ignore. It is ghosted before it
        leaves your machine, so what the rest of the table receives has no dice, no total and no
        reading in it — only that you rolled. The same is true of the history a late arrival is sent.
        Your own screen keeps the numbers, because the secret is from the table and not from you.
    </p>
    <p>
        The table code is the whole of the security: twelve random characters that nobody will guess,
        and that anybody you send them to can use. Treat the link like a key. And because the
        connection is direct, a strict firewall between two players can refuse to allow one at all.
    </p>
</section>

<section class="prose">
    <h2>Talents, and why there are none</h2>
    <p>
        A To Hit roll reports what the dice show as a poker hand — pairs, triplets, full houses,
        sequences — because that is what talents, legendary weapons and most of the bestiary are
        keyed to. A Cutthroat who rolls two pairs attacks again; the table will now say so instead of
        leaving it to whoever was looking closely.
    </p>
    <p>
        What the tool will not do is apply it. Encoding every talent would mean a rules engine to
        maintain, an answer to give whenever a talent is ambiguous about when it fires, and a
        disagreement with every table that house-ruled one. The dice are read honestly and the rest
        is your character sheet, which is the part you came for.
    </p>
</section>

@code {
    [CascadingParameter]
    public GameData Data { get; set; } = default!;

    private UiText Ui => Data.Ui;
}
